<h2>The arguments that persuaded the Court to look again</h2>
<p>The revisionist pressed three connected points: no independent witness supported the recovery; the articles allegedly recovered from him were never identified as the stolen articles; and in the absence of that identification there was no evidentiary link between him and the offence of dishonestly receiving stolen property. The State, significantly, did not dispute that more than half of the one-year sentence already stood served. The Court was careful not to pronounce on the merits — the order records only that the matter requires scrutiny — but the combination of a short residual sentence and an arguable evidentiary gap made suspension of sentence the only order that would keep the revision alive as a real remedy.</p>

<h2>Why suspension was the pivotal relief</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Bearing on suspension</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Sentence of one year, over half served</td><td>Full service likely before the revision could be heard — the remedy would become academic</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Concurrent findings of two courts</td><td>Raises the threshold for interference, but does not bar admission where scrutiny is warranted</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Identification of recovered property in doubt</td><td>Goes to the root of a Section 411 conviction</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Custody-caused delay of fourteen days</td><td>Condoned as sufficient cause</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical significance</h2>
<p>For convicted persons serving short sentences, this order maps the realistic route after a first appeal fails: an urgent revision with a delay-condonation application that squarely pleads incarceration, a focused attack on one or two foundational defects rather than a wholesale re-argument of facts, and a prayer for suspension of sentence that explains why time will defeat the remedy. For the prosecution, the lesson sits at the trial stage — in property offences, formal identification of the recovered articles by the complainant or witnesses is not a ritual but the connective tissue of the charge, and its absence follows the case file all the way up. The revision itself remains pending; the findings of the courts below stand, suspended but undisturbed, until the High Court examines the summoned record.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> A revision is not a second appeal. Counsel drafting one should frame grounds in the language of perversity and legal error, not sufficiency of evidence — the former invites the supervisory jurisdiction, the latter invites dismissal at the threshold.</p>
</div>
